1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,400
So the ugly truth of smartphones is finally out.

2
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:24,120
What leading thinkers including Simon Sinek and Jonathan Haidt have been signaling for a few years

3
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:29,220
has now been proven out with the latest research from Sapien Labs.

4
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:36,840
In a study where 28,000 young adults, those between 18 to 24 years of age,

5
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:41,220
were asked to complete the MHQ, the mental health quotient,

6
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:43,740
along with a question that went,

7
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:52,120
at what age did you get your first smartphone or tablet with internet access that you could carry with you?

8
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:56,100
For me, there were four findings that really stood out.

9
00:00:56,100 --> 00:01:04,320
First, mental well-being improved with every subsequently older age of smartphone ownership.

10
00:01:04,860 --> 00:01:11,400
Second, females were consistently more impacted than males at a young age.

11
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:16,700
Third, the social self, which is how we see ourselves in relationship to the others,

12
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:23,620
and this is the dimension that has been most impacted with the highest drop between older generations to younger ones,

13
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,640
saw the biggest improvement with every older age of smartphone acquisition.

14
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:38,700
And finally, four, suicidal thoughts and tendencies of feeling aggression towards the others

15
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:46,260
also consistently improved with every older year in which people got to acquire their smartphones.

16
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:52,760
Interestingly, the age of smartphone ownership was lower in the developed world.

17
00:01:53,220 --> 00:01:57,540
North America, Europe, and Australia, kids were getting, on average,

18
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
a smartphone between 11.5 to 12.5 years,

19
00:02:01,420 --> 00:02:04,260
whereas in places like Africa or South Asia,

20
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:09,000
the average age of first smartphone ownership was between 16 and 17.

21
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,980
So what does this mean for us?

22
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:17,140
If you're a parent, an educator, a lawmaker, a policymaker,

23
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:22,080
I think this research from Sapien Labs is a major call to action.

24
00:02:22,660 --> 00:02:27,700
For me, I would think about smartphones and children

25
00:02:27,700 --> 00:02:30,640
pretty much the way we think about children and driving.

26
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:32,840
In most countries around the world,

27
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:36,420
you need to be of a certain age, 15 years or more,

28
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:38,700
before you can be given a car key.

29
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:44,680
You need to take a theory lesson to understand the safety rules and traffic rules

30
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:47,620
and get instructed by a trained expert.

31
00:02:48,380 --> 00:02:51,280
Perhaps clock a number of hours under supervision

32
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:54,920
from someone that has a driving license next to you.

33
00:02:55,260 --> 00:02:57,060
And finally, you've got to pass a test

34
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:00,340
before you can get out on the road and be by yourself.

35
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:05,880
So why wouldn't we think about smartphones in a similar way

36
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:11,060
rather than just handing young kids these devices

37
00:03:11,060 --> 00:03:14,500
that can be so harmful if not used properly?

38
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:16,960
Shouldn't we bring in regulations

39
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:22,800
and systemically help introduce our children to this world of technology

40
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:26,960
so that them and all the other generations that follow

41
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:30,080
can actually start to enhance their well-being?

42
